From 7a08980d80d1ebac2c001dce3feb178cfd1c147b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: konkanello <konkanello@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:41:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mimicos: let MimicOS own memory management in
 syscall-emulation mode

SE mode allocates physical frames with a bump pointer and resolves
minor faults functionally, in zero simulated time.  Neither models
anything: placement is whatever order pages happened to be touched in,
and a fault costs whatever number is in a config file.  Studies of
allocator policy, fragmentation or huge pages cannot be run in SE mode
at all.

This hands memory management to MimicOS.  Opt-in and inert by default:
without MIMICOS_HOME the MimicOS files compile to stubs and gem5 is
unchanged.

  MIMICOS_HOME=/path/to/mimicos scons build/X86/gem5.opt

  build/X86/gem5.opt configs/example/mimicos_se.py \
      --mimicos-config <ini> [--mimicos-server <server binary>] <app>

What it adds:

  Allocation.  Process::allocateMem resolves one page at a time through
  MimicOS, which places frames from the virtual address.  MimicOS's
  space is reserved out of the MemPool up front so its frames cannot
  collide with the ones gem5 hands out for its own structures.

  Two page sizes in the TLB.  The trie already supports them for full
  system mode; SE mode was passing the full key width and hardcoding
  logBytes, so a 2M page cost 512 entries and bought nothing.  The page
  table now records the real page size and the TLB installs one entry
  for it.  A 32 MB working set walked four times goes from 32796 data
  TLB read misses to 2.

  A kernel in the guest.  With --mimicos-server, MimicOS runs as an
  ordinary process on its own CPU and the simulator hands it each
  fault over three m5ops: wait, asid, reply.  Its allocator and page
  tables execute as guest instructions, so a fault costs what the
  handler does rather than what a config file says.  Any number of
  threads may fault at once; they queue and each is woken by its own
  answer.

Things that had to be right, and were not at first:

  A fault takes two trips through the handler.  The first parks the
  thread, the second goes down the ordinary fixupFault path so the
  region checks in MemState still run; only the frame comes from the
  kernel.  Resolving it directly would let a bad access be mapped
  instead of faulting.

  Never fall back quietly.  An early version declined to hand over a
  fault while the kernel was still starting, and the run used gem5's
  own allocator throughout while looking perfectly healthy.

  Park, do not spin.  suspend() lets the pseudo instruction return and
  the thread keeps fetching; quiesce() is what m5_quiesce uses.

  The kernel faults too, on its own text and stack, and cannot serve
  those itself.  It is identified by pid and its faults take the normal
  path.

  A large TLB entry answers for its whole region, but the binary's text
  and data are mapped before the kernel is running, out of gem5's pool.
  The entry was redirecting those reads into the large frame and the
  program read someone else's memory.  A large entry is now installed
  only when the whole region is backed by that one allocation.

Measured with the kernel in the guest, a 2 MB working set: 516 faults
served at 4K, 6 at 2M with four large pages, 516 mixed, each ending on
its own with the workload's checksum intact.  Four threads over 4 MB:
1037 faults, up to three waiting at once, checksum intact.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0198h1jxpi9UhkKyfhM3h2gH
